        "title": "[codex] add native desktop apps for macOS, Windows, and Linux",
        "body": "## What\n\nAdds a first-party Electron desktop shell for ClickClack on macOS, Windows, and Linux. The app keeps the hosted/self-hosted web client as the product surface, then adds a narrow native bridge for capabilities browsers cannot provide reliably.\n\n## Why a native shell\n\nThis is not a separate chat implementation. The web client remains canonical; the shell enriches it with operating-system integration:\n\n- native notifications that deep-link to the relevant channel or DM\n- Dock/taskbar badges, Windows overlay badge, and tray unread state\n- close-to-tray background presence with realtime activity kept alive\n- global app-menu shortcut for Quick Compose (`Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+K`)\n- `clickclack://` deep links with cold-start and single-instance routing\n- native download handling, external-link routing, spellcheck, and context menus\n- launch-at-login controls on macOS and Windows\n- persistent window placement plus a native settings window\n- hosted ClickClack by default, with HTTPS self-hosted server configuration and loopback HTTP for local development\n\n## Desktop architecture\n\n- Adds `@clickclack/desktop` under `apps/desktop` with separate main-app and settings preloads.\n- Keeps context isolation, sandboxing, web security, and Node integration boundaries enabled.\n- Exposes a small typed bridge for notifications, unread counts, active routes, settings, and Quick Compose only.\n- Restricts the main renderer to the configured ClickClack origin; external navigation opens in the system browser.\n- Runs GitHub OAuth in the main app window, while exact-origin preload gating and IPC sender checks withhold every native desktop capability from GitHub pages.\n- Validates server origins, app routes, notifications, and IPC senders before native actions.\n- Stores desktop settings atomically in Electron's user-data directory, with user-only file permissions on Unix.\n\n## Packaging and identity\n\n- macOS: DMG and ZIP, Intel and Apple Silicon\n- Windows: NSIS installer and ZIP, x64\n- Linux: AppImage and DEB, x64\n- Adds a new cross-platform ClickClack icon: coral tile, opposing cream claws, and an aqua activity pulse, with generated ICNS/ICO/PNG assets plus tray artwork.\n- Adds a GitHub Actions matrix that tests, type-checks, packages, and uploads unsigned preview artifacts on all three operating systems.\n\n## Web integration and docs\n\n- Connects realtime unread state, active routes, deep-link navigation, Quick Compose focus, and notification delivery to the desktop bridge.\n- Preserves browser notification behavior outside Electron.\n- Adds desktop install, development, security, packaging, and native-feature documentation.\n- Adds the user-facing changelog entry.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `pnpm check`\n- `pnpm build`\n- `pnpm fmt:check`\n- `pnpm test:e2e` — 41 passed\n- `actionlint .github/workflows/desktop.yml`\n- `CSC_IDENTITY_AUTO_DISCOVERY=false pnpm --filter @clickclack/desktop run pack`\n- Packaged macOS app behavior pass: launch, hosted sign-in surface, server validation, insecure remote HTTP rejection, close-to-tray, and restore\n- Computer-controlled packaged-app OAuth pass: the existing ClickClack window transforms into GitHub sign-in with no child window; a `clickclack://` deep link during OAuth reclaims that same window and restores the app.\n- Structured Codex autoreview: clean, no accepted/actionable findings\n\n## Deliberate follow-up\n\nCI artifacts are unsigned previews. Production distribution still needs platform signing credentials, macOS notarization, and a signed update-channel design; this PR does not invent those release contracts.\n",
